@page page_faq_home FAQs and Troubleshooting (User Perspective)

<!-- Add this one for href links to subitems -->
\tableofcontents

Some frequently asked questions and problems are discussed below.

# CMake

- ***How can I tell the code generator which build folder to use?***

The build system is CMake only. By default, the Python code generator searches
upwards from the current working directory for a `PeanoConfig.cmake` file and
uses the build folder containing it. If there are multiple CMake build folders
present (e.g. a GPU build and a non-GPU build), set the environment variable
`PEANO_BUILD_DIR` (or its alias `PEANO_CMAKE_BUILD_DIR`) to the build folder you
want the code generator to use; `PEANO_BUILD_DIR` takes precedence if both are
set. Unsetting the environment variable reverts to the automatic search.

# Compilation

- ***The translation fails with the error***
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  '#pragma float_control(precise, off)' is illegal when except is enabled
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  We have seen this error with some LLVM-based compilers. Please re-translate with ```-fno-exceptions```.
  Unfortunately, this disables some error checking in the checkpoint and file output routines.
  If that does not help, you might want to reduce the optimisation level. On
  Intel, switching from -fast to -O3 does work in some cases.

- ***My C++ version is too old***
  Peano requires C++20, which CMake enforces automatically. If your compiler
  defaults to an older standard, you can force the flag through
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
cmake .. -DPEANO_EXTRA_CXX_FLAGS="-std=c++20"
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  Depending on your compiler, the flag might also be
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
cmake .. -DPEANO_EXTRA_CXX_FLAGS="--std=c++20"
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  Some compilers want you to use two hyphens, others expect one. Most
  Clang-based compiler installations do not bring their own C++ standard
  library to the table. They use the pre-existing GCC STL. Now, if your
  Clang-based compiler - that includes NVIDIA and Intel - is built against a
  version of GNU whose STL is too old, you will get errors despite the std
  flag. In this case, you have to load a newer GCC module *after* you have
  loaded your Intel, NVIDIA or Clang module. You load the Clang-based
  compiler which will introduce a GNU STL, i.e. set the corresponding environment
  variables. After that, you manually load a newer GCC which redirects all of
  these variables.

  If there are multiple `gcc` versions present, it is possible for CLANG to
  automatically select an outdated version thus creates problem when
  compiling. You can check what is the selection version with `clang++ -v`.
  Use this to select the other listed options:
  `--gcc-install-dir=/path/to/new/gcc`.

- ***My compiler reports***
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
error: `align_val_t` is not a member of `std`
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  We have seen this error with GCC if the supported C++ generation is too old.
  See remarks above.

- ***My compiler yields errors like***
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
`warning #3191: scoped enumeration types are a C++11 feature`
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  Your compiler seems not to have
  C++11 (or, newer) enabled by default. See remarks above.

- ***My compiler fails with***
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
error: name followed by "::" must be a class or namespace name
        if (not std::filesystem::exists(indexFileName + ".pvd"))
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  We have seen this error with GCC if the supported C++ generation is too old.
  See remarks above.

- ***My compiler terminates with***
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
`error: unknown attribute 'optimize' ignored*`
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
   We have seen this issue on macOS
   with CLANG replacing GCC. Unfortunately, CLANG seems to pretend to be GNU on
   some systems and then the wrong header is included. Consult the remarks on
   @ref page_compiler_specific_settings_home "compiler-specific settings". Select
   the Clang toolchain explicitly via
   ```cmake .. -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=clang++ -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=clang``` so that
   Peano's compiler-specific settings header picks up the correct compiler.

- ***My compiler terminates with errors when I enable OpenMP multithreading***
  Use a compiler with OpenMP 5.0 support, such as GCC 9.x.

- ***I configured Peano with HDF5, but my application (experiment or benchmark) will not compile***
  HDF5 support is enabled through the CMake option `PEANO_WITH_HDF5` (on by
  default). Peano links only the HDF5 C component (`find_package(HDF5 COMPONENTS C)`);
  there is no dependency on the HDF5 C++ binding. The resulting include and link
  flags are attached to the `PeanoOptions` interface target and written into
  `PeanoConfig.cmake`, which the Python API reads to generate the makefile for
  your application. If HDF5 is not picked up, reconfigure your build with
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
cmake .. -DPEANO_WITH_HDF5=ON
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  and re-run your Python scripts so they regenerate the makefile from the
  updated configuration. If HDF5 lives in a non-standard location, point CMake
  at it via the standard `-DHDF5_ROOT=/path/to/hdf5` hint.

- ***The configuration fails for the NVIDIA compiler*** For NVIDIA, we have to
  make both the C and the C++ compiler point to the C++ compiler `nvc++`, as
  both are passed the same arguments which are actually only understood by the
  C++ version. So please point both compiler switches to nvc++.
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
export NVCPP=/opt/nvidia/hpc_sdk/Linux_x86_64/2022/compilers/bin/nvc++
cmake .. -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=$NVCPP -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=$NVCPP ...
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  Furthermore, some compute kernels are not available with the NVIDIA tools, as the compiler is
  pretty picky when it decides which temporary variables it might place on the
  call stack. This should not affect Peano 4's core, but it can affect some
  extensions such as ExaHyPE.

- ***My compiler stops with ***

  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  /apps/developers/compilers/gcc/13.2/1/default/lib/gcc/x86_64-pc-linux-gnu/13.2.0/../../../../include/c++/13.2.0/chrono:2320:48: error: call to consteval function 'std::chrono::hh_mm_ss::_S_fractional_width' is not a constant expression
        static constexpr unsigned fractional_width = {_S_fractional_width()};
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  We have seen this with too ***new*** C++ standard libs such as the version 13.2.
  Use an older one such as 11.2.

- ***With Intel's OpenMP, the link fails with an undefined reference to
  `__kmpc_omp_taskwait_deps_51` in the tasking layer***

  We have seen this with Intel compiler when you use the Clang LLVM OpenMP
  runtime, i.e. use -fopenmp. Switch to -fiopenmp instead.


# Linking

- ***The linking fails for the tutorials even though I've followed all instructions***
  Almost always, this is due to missing packages. You have to configure your
  build with all packages a given tutorial relies on. Return to your build
  directory and re-run CMake (or edit the options interactively via `ccmake ..`)
  toggling on all `PEANO_WITH_*` options required, then rebuild with `make -j`.

- ***My linker complains about a missing tbb, even though I do not use Intel's TBB***
  Intel has donated TBB to the open source community and Clang, for example, uses
  it internally to realise its OpenMP scheduler. Unfortunately, the integration
  is sometimes not mature, i.e. the compiler then does not automatically add
  the library to its settings. Reconfigure with
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
cmake .. -DPEANO_EXTRA_LINK_FLAGS="-ltbb"
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  and you should be fine.

- ***If I use Fortran as well, I get errors alike***
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
undefined reference to for_stop_core
undefined reference to for_write_seq_list
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  This tells you
  that the linker failed to find the Fortran standard libraries. For the GNU
  compilers, you need for example the flag -lgfortran while Intel requires
  -lifcore. Pass them to your build via `-DPEANO_EXTRA_LINK_FLAGS="-lgfortran"`
  (or `-lifcore`).
  You can add these flags manually via your Python script to the code, but
  this is a flaw. They belong into the build configuration, not into your
  Python script.

- ***The linker complains about some routines/classes from the technical
  architectures, but they are definitely there***
  GCC/C++ is extremely sensitive when it comes to the order of the libraries
  used. You might have to edit your makefile manually to get a "valid"
  ordering that works. We recommend that you always link from the most
  abstract library to the lowest level lib. In one ExaHyPE project, e.g., the
  following order worked whereas all others resulted in linker errors:
  -lExaHyPEAsserts2D, -lPeanoAsserts2D, -lTarchAsserts.
  So the link order is high-level extensions, core, tarch. The
  library name encodes the build mode (Release, Debug, Asserts) and the
  dimension. The Makefile which is generated by the Python API should follow
  this convention.

# Runtime

  - ***My unit tests fail in the StackTopologyTest***. We have found that GCC's
    STL is buggy (confirmed in version 9.3.0), although we do not know if this bug
    is found within the unordered_set, the bitset or the
    pair implementation. Anyway, a newer version of GCC (12 for example)
    seem to fix this issue. If you use Intel's toolchain or literally any LLVM,
    you might have to manually load a newer GNU version after you've loaded your
    core compiler.

  - ***sycl-ls shows OpenCL devices and no Level Zero device (Intel PVC)*** This can happen if the following environment variable was set ZET_ENABLE_PROGRAM_DEBUGGING=1. Unset it to fix the issue.


# Python API

-   ***`import exahype2` yields not found error***. You haven't set your
    `PYTHONPATH` properly. It has to point to Peano's api
    subdirectory. So use something similar to

             export PYTHONPATH=~/git/Peano/api

-   My installation or paraview complains about ***ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'numpy'***.
    For some parts of Peano and its extensions, you need the package
    numpy. If you don't
    have numpy on your system install it with either of the following
    commands:

             pip3 install --user numpy

    Similar arguments hold for jinja2. Peano's API and all extensions can work without
    numpy in principle. However, not all features (solvers in ExaHyPE, e.g.)
    might be available if you have no numpy.

- ***A formatting script reports that a tool is not installed***. Install the tools
  with ``pip install -r requirements.txt``; the scripts find them on the ``PATH``.

# Performance analysis

- ***The Intel/NVIDIA tools do not provide meaningful insight*** Read through
  the vendor-specific \ref page_compiler_specific_settings_home "supercomputer/tool remarks"
  and pick the vendor-specific built toolchain. After that, profile the code in
  the profile version.
  The release builds of the libraries do not yield much additional information
  for spotting runtime flaws.
- ***The Intel/NVIDIA tools yield too much data*** First, pick the
  \ref page_compiler_specific_settings_home "vendor-specific toolchain" fitting to your system/compiler. After that,
  amend your log filter. See \ref tarch_logging for more info.


# Debugging

- ***I can't see my log/trace statements.*** There's a whole set of
  things that can go wrong.
  -   Translate your main code with a reasonable debug
      flag. The makefile has to translate your code with
      `-DPEANO_DEBUG=1`.
  -   Link to libraries which are built with a reasonable
      diagnostic switch.


# MPI troubleshooting

- ***My code crashes unexpectedly with malloc/free errors***. Please
  rerun your code with Intel MPI and the flag `-check_mpi`. You should
  not get any error reports. If you do, we have had serious problems
  as some of Peano's classes use `MPI_C_BOOL` or `MPI_CXX_BOOL`. They
  seem not be supported properly.

- ***With more and more ranks, I suddenly run into timeouts***. Peano has a
  built-in deadlock detector with a default threshold of 600 seconds inside one
  wait. Use `--deadlockTimeout` to raise the threshold or disable it.


C++ core
--------

-   **Where is the information whether a point or face is at the
    boundary or not?** Peano does not provide any such feature. Many codes for
    example use the grid to represent complicated domains, and thus
    would need such a feature anyway. So what you have to do is to add a
    bool to each vertex/face and set this boolean yourself.


Parallelisation
---------------

-   **Why does adaptive refinement need several sweeps?** A refinement
    request names a cell that already exists and can expose one additional
    level. The next criterion evaluation can then request cells on that new
    level. A regrid also uses a transition sweep to create and destroy entities
    while it migrates persistent data into the target rank ownership.

-   **Why can the rank partition remain imbalanced?** Peano assigns complete
    spawn-level blocks, or the children of a heavy block, using leaf-cell
    weights. An indivisible heavy unit limits the attainable balance, and
    equal leaf weights do not represent solver-specific work such as limiters
    or particle interactions.

-   **Where is the load-balancer configuration?** Rank partitioning is part of
    the spacetree engine. Every rank derives the same ownership table from the
    rank count and refinement plan; an application does not select a separate
    load-balancer object.

-   **I get a timeout but Peano claims that there are still messages in the
    MPI queue. How do I know what certain MPI messages tags mean?** All
    tags used in Peano are registered through the operation
    `tarch::mpi::Rank::reserveFreeTag`. If you translate with
    `PEANO_DEBUG=1`, then you get a list of tag semantics at
    startup. These lists are the same as the ones you use in release
    mode, i.e. when this information is not dumped. The
    info goes straight to cout, i.e. you can't filter it. That's because
    the tag allocation usually happens before our logging landscape is
    up and configured properly.

-   **I have introduced auxiliary (material) parameters and
    the code crashes every now and then.** Initialise all data in your
    code. That includes any potential
    auxiliary variables. We usually use

    for (int i=0; i<NumberOfUnknowns+NumberOfAuxiliaryVariables; i++)
      Q[i] = 0.0;

    as a first step within `initialCondition()`. This leaves no garbage
    in the data. Further to that,
    also set proper boundary data for any auxiliary value. You might not
    need these data, as you do not use the auxiliary variables to
    exchange information between patches, but you have to set proper
    values nevertheless. Otherwise, all of Peano's assertions will fail as it
    detects data corruption.

-   **My higher order solver yields physically slightly unreasonable
    data for the Riemann solves.** If you use a higher-order scheme Peano has
    to project your polynomial solution onto the faces. For this, it
    uses pre-assembled matrices which are stored in double precision.
    However, round-off errors might lead to situations where you get
    small deviations---we have seen densities around $-10^{-8}$ for the
    Euler equations, e.g. Usually, such round-off errors are fine, but
    you might run into problems if you take the square root of such tiny
    negative values, e.g. It is therefore important that you apply the
    maximum function:

    // Naive code leading into problems:
    assertion(tarch::la::greaterEquals(p,0.0), p);
    const double c = std::sqrt( gamma * p * irho );

    // working code:
    const double c = std::sqrt(std::max(0.0,gamma * p * irho));

*/
